  "checkpoints": [
    {
      "at": 0,
      "title": "Find a binding ceiling",
      "body": "The red ceiling line is below the equilibrium point E. Because the legal price is below equilibrium, the ceiling binds."
    },
    {
      "at": 2.5,
      "title": "Read the gap",
      "body": "At the ceiling, follow the green supply curve to Qs and the blue demand curve to Qd. Qd is to the right of Qs, so quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied."
    },
    {
      "at": 5,
      "title": "Shortage, not zero supply",
      "body": "Shortage equals Qd minus Qs. Sellers still supply Qs, so a shortage does not mean zero supply. Some buyers gain, but others cannot buy, and total surplus does not rise."
    }
  ],
  "narration": [
    {
      "at": 0,
      "text": "A price ceiling is a legal maximum price. It binds only when it is set below the equilibrium price, as this red line is."
    },
    {
      "at": 2.7,
      "text": "At the ceiling, the quantity demanded Qd is larger than the quantity supplied Qs. The red arrow between them is the shortage."
    },
    {
      "at": 5.4,
      "text": "Shortage is Qd minus Qs, not zero supply. A ceiling helps some buyers but leaves others unable to buy, and it does not increase total surplus."
    }
  ],
  "fallback": {
    "text": "A price ceiling is a maximum legal price. It binds when set below equilibrium. At a binding ceiling, quantity demanded Qd exceeds quantity supplied Qs, so the shortage is Qd minus Qs. Sellers still supply a positive Qs. Some buyers gain, but others cannot buy, and price controls do not raise total surplus."
  },
